## 3. Shortest reasonable path for each critical task

An *interaction* is a click, tap or key press that commits an action. Reading a screen is not counted.

| Task | Shortest path | Interactions | Target (from success criteria) | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **T1** Generate and confirm route | Dashboard (landing, 0) then **Select all pending** (1), **Generate Route** (2), review on Route Review, **Confirm and Assign** (3) | 3 | 5 or fewer | Proposed driver is pre-filled on Route Review. Changing the driver adds 1-2. A shift-hours warning adds 1 (drop suggested stop). Choosing only some deliveries replaces "select all" with a filter plus select-all (2) or individual ticks |
| **T2** Record delivery outcome | My Route (landing, 0) then **tap delivery** (1), **tap status** (2) | 2 | 2 | Meets the Usability scenario. Undo replaces a confirmation dialog, so no third tap |
| **T3** Reassign active route | **Active Routes** in top nav (1), **Reassign** row action (2), **choose driver** (3), **Confirm** (4) | 4 | 4 or fewer | Row action avoids opening Route Detail first (which would be 5). Dialog shows available drivers and a from/to summary |
| Reorder waypoints (FR-02) | Active Routes (1), open route (2), move stop (3), Save (4) | 4 | not critical | Move up/down buttons must exist alongside any drag option (keyboard access) |
| Daily report (FR-07) | Reports (1) | 1 | not critical | Top nav link |

## 4. Design rationale

**Choice 1: task-first landing screens.** The Dispatcher lands on pending deliveries and generates a route from there; the driver lands directly on their current route. Each role starts at the screen where its most frequent task begins, giving the 3- and 2-interaction paths above.
*Realistic alternative rejected:* a generic home page with menu items such as "Create Route". It adds a navigation step before every morning dispatch and is the kind of decorative home page the lab warns against.
*Consequence accepted:* the pending list may be long (50 or more deliveries), so the dashboard needs filtering and "select all" to stay fast. Wireframes (block 44-76) must show these.

**Choice 2: flat top navigation, maximum depth 2 for the Dispatcher.** Four top-level items (Dashboard, Active Routes, Drivers, Reports) are visible on every screen.
*Realistic alternative rejected:* a side menu with nested groups. Nesting is unnecessary for four sections and hides items from a user working under peak pressure.
*Consequence accepted:* adding more sections later (for example a settings area) will require rethinking the top bar.

**Choice 3: two role-specific client views over one backend.** A desktop-oriented Dispatcher view and a mobile-first Driver view call the same API.
*Realistic alternative rejected:* one responsive UI for both roles. It would put dispatcher functions in the driver's pocket and complicate the 2-tap driver task.
*Consequence accepted:* two sets of screens to design, build and test.

## 5. Consistency notes (UI words versus domain words)

| Shown to users | Domain / API name |
| --- | --- |
| Route | `RouteManifest` (`route-manifests` in the API) |
| Delivery or stop | `DeliveryNode` |
| Driver | `Driver` |
| Draft route | `RouteManifest` with status `DRAFT` (or `REQUIRES_REVIEW` when shift hours are exceeded) |
| Active route | `RouteManifest` with status `ACTIVE` |

- Route Review corresponds to the `201` response of the generate operation; Confirm and Assign corresponds to the separate confirm operation named in the API contract.
